# ── Build-process litter ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every one of these has been committed to this repo at least once, usually by
# uploading a file through the GitHub web UI — which does NOT honour .gitignore.
# Keeping the rules anyway so local `git add -A` stays safe.
*.patch
*.diff
apply-*.sh
*.zip
.backups/

# The npm lockfile is not used here — pnpm-lock.yaml is authoritative.
package-lock.json

# Patch files are a transport format, not source.
#
# FOUR of them were committed to the repository root: 0082truthcontrolprobe.patch,
# 0001searchai20260824.patch, ecowoodsseov2.patch and ecowoods_seo_v3.patch. The last two are this
# branch's own patches, uploaded through the GitHub web UI — which silently
# strips the dashes from a filename, which is why `git am 0001-search-ai-...`
# and `git am ecowoods-seo-v2.patch` both failed with "No such file or
# directory" while the file sat in the directory the command was run from.
#
# If you are here because a patch will not apply: `ls *.patch` first. The name
# on disk is not the name it was sent under.
#
# A committed patch is a second, frozen copy of changes that also exist in the
# history, and it will drift from them the moment anything is amended. It is
# also 500 KB of diff text that every clone, every CI checkout and every crawler
# of the raw repository now carries forever. Deliver a patch as a file; apply it;
# delete it.
*.patch

# audit-rendered writes a report per run, against whatever host it was pointed
# at. Committing it means the repo carries one person's measurement of one
# deployment as if it were a fact about the code.
audit/rendered.json
audit/rendered.md

# crawl-site.mjs is the same thing and was missed when that rule was written:
# it fetches every route from a LIVE host and writes what it found, so the file
# is a snapshot of a deployment at a moment, not a property of this repository.
# Two people crawling on the same day would commit different files and neither
# would be wrong. Run it, read it, throw it away — the report that matters is
# the exit code.
audit/site-crawl.json
audit/site-crawl.csv
audit/site-crawl.md

# Development-only assessment photographs. lib/floor-graph/photo-storage.ts
# writes here when no private Supabase bucket is configured, deliberately
# OUTSIDE public/ so a local run cannot serve somebody's living room as a
# static asset. Never committed.
.floor-graph/
*.zip

# ── The build-time public/ sync ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Vercel serves the REPO-ROOT public/ as the static root, not apps/web/public —
# see docs/PUBLIC_ASSETS.md. vercel.json's buildCommand copies the app's public
# directory up at build time, which means a local `pnpm build` leaves 400+
# duplicated files here. They are build output, not source: ignore everything
# except the handful that genuinely live at the root.
/public/*
!/public/brand/
!/public/qr-app.jpg
!/public/review-card.svg
